    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we can multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator.\n\nContent:\nSo, to divide [MATH: 2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5},] we multiply the [MATH: 2] by [MATH: {\\color{blue}5}\\!:]\n[MATH: 2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = 2 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10]\n\nContent:\nAnd that's the answer! Notice that this method does the same job as the fraction model method we used before:\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/82c7aad30359cbdefa306c8c2eb5226c]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Dividing a Whole Number by a Unit Fraction When the Denominator has One Digit\n\nExample Prompt:\nDivide [MATH: 14 \\div \\dfrac{1}{3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n[MATH: 14 \\div \\dfrac{1}{3} = 14 \\times 3 = 42]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5 \u00f7 (1)/(2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: 10]\nc. [MATH: 7]\nd. [MATH: 3]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(10)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 16 \u00f7 (1)/(3) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(48)]\nb. [MATH: 48]\nc. [MATH: (16)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: 13]\ne. [MATH: 19]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Dividing a Whole Number by a Unit Fraction When the Denominator has Two Digits\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: 5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{15} ?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n[MATH: 5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{15} = 5 \\times 15 = 75]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2 \u00f7 (1)/(10) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 20]\nb. [MATH: 6]\nc. [MATH: 12]\nd. [MATH: 15]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 4 \u00f7 (1)/(11) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 44]\nb. [MATH: (11)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: 22]\nd. [MATH: (4)/(11)]\ne. [MATH: 15]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions: Word Problems\n\nExample Prompt:\nJohnny has [MATH: 5] kilograms of wheat flour with which he wants to make some pizzas. If each pizza needs [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{10}] of a kilogram of wheat flour, how many pizzas can Johnny make?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo determine the number of pizzas that Johnny can make, we divide the amount of wheat flour by the amount of wheat flour each pizza needs.\n[MATH: 5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{10}]\n\nTo divide a whole number by a unit fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator:\n[MATH: 5 \\div \\dfrac{1}{10} = 5 \\times 10 = 50]\n\nTherefore, Johnny can make [MATH: 50] pizzas.\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nAna has [MATH: 3] kilograms of wheat flour with which she wants to make some cakes. If each cake needs [MATH: (1)/(2)] of a kilogram of wheat flour, how many cakes can Ana make?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10]\nb. [MATH: 6]\nc. [MATH: 5]\nd. [MATH: 7]\ne. [MATH: 8]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nWilliam is filling a [MATH: 3] liter bottle with water using a cup. How many cups of water does William need to use to fill the bottle if the capacity of one cup is [MATH: (1)/(6)] of a liter?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 12]\nb. [MATH: 18]\nc. [MATH: 9]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(18)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(2)]"
